HEY - LISTEN - This is approximately what scmgr said:

I thought you would like to have this (or look at it for ideas on
other scripts).  Put this in your path, change a few things (noted
below), and voila... you have a trashcan script that will delete the
oldest "n" number of files in the trashcan directory upon logging
out.
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
#! /bin/csh -f
onintr QUIT
set command = $0
set command = $command:t
set workdir = ~/,trashcan      ****** change to whatever "trash" dir you want
set tmp = /usr/tmp/${command}$$

######################
# Removing old files #
######################
cd $workdir
ls -at|sed '1,30d'>$tmp.1   ****** change "30" for MAX # files to keep in trash
if ( `cat $tmp.1|wc -l` > 0 ) then
   xargs /bin/rm -fr < $tmp.1
endif

QUIT:
/bin/rm -fr $tmp.* > /dev/null
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

Add a line to run this script in you ".logout" file... something like:

( ~/,data/,per/,prog/.trash_removal & ) >& /dev/null


Also, alias the "rm" command to be equal to something like:

                     "mv \!:* ~/,trashcan"

You get the idea... you can "undelete" up to "n" number of files - as
long as you do so before deleting "n" (or a number you specify) other
files.

